{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791604436,"nonce":"UeEBRIpFCAwGEdIoGzUNouZJK3Q=","title":"Lua GC: gc/weak_value_keys keeps a cleared holder's values in about 2% of stressed debug runs","message":"conformance gc_stressed failed once in a full `cargo test -p zyntax_lua --features llvm-backend` run on the Mac (2026-10-10). The case is gc/weak_value_keys.lua; the failing output is\n\n    after 2\t5\t5\n    after 3\t5\t5\n\nwhere the reference prints `after 2\t0\t5` and `after 3\t0\t0`: after `holder.held = nil`, `scrub(200)` and `collectgarbage()`, the five values that holder.held kept alive (ids 6 to 10) are still reached, and stay reached through the next collection too. A single word naming holder.held's table, or its entry storage, would hold all five.\n\nRate, ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1 ZYNTAX_GC_FLOOR_KB=1, debug llvm-backend binaries, six parallel loops alternating the two binaries on the same machine and load:\n- built at 5bc7c549 (before the HirType Arc change): 5 of 240\n- with the Arc change: 4 of 240\n- release binary, unloaded: 0 of 6 (all gc cases)\n\nEvery failure is the same output. It needs no particular commit to show up; whether the stale word is in a stack frame or in a block reached from one is not yet known. Next step: under ZYNTAX_GC_EXPLAIN=1, report how each value of a weak-value table that survives a program collection was reached (host_gc.rs report_survivors covers only finalizable objects and keys of nil values), then clear whatever holds the word. Related: 63b3457 (a stale word keeping a weak-key table's dead keys), 558e28e.","files":null}]}